So the nullability of Game.loop() is exactly the nullability of the static field `gameLoop`.

From the provided `Game` class:

- In `init`:

```java
public static synchronized void init(String... args) {
  if (initialized) {
    ...
    return;
  }

  ...
  gameLoop = new GameLoop("Main Update Loop", config().client().getMaxFps());
  IGameLoop loop = loop();
  if (loop != null) {
    loop.attach(physics());
    loop.attach(world());
  }
  ...
  initialized = true;
}
```

Here `gameLoop` is explicitly set to a new `GameLoop(...)` before `initialized` is set to true. So after a successful `init` call, `gameLoop` is non‑null.

- In `start`:

```java
public static synchronized void start() {
  if (!initialized) {
    throw new IllegalStateException(
        "The game cannot be started without being first initialized. Call Game.init(...) before Game.start().");
  }

  gameLoop.start();
  ...
  hasStarted = true;
}
```

`start` assumes `gameLoop` is non‑null (calls `start()` on it). This is consistent with the guarantee from `init`.

- In `terminate`:

```java
static synchronized void terminate() {
  if (!initialized) {
    return;
  }

  hasStarted = false;
  initialized = false;

  config().save();
  gameLoop.terminate();
  ...
  gameLoop = null;
  ...
}
```

`gameLoop` is explicitly set to `null` at the end of `terminate`.

There is no other assignment to `gameLoop` in the snippets besides `init` (assign non‑null) and `terminate` (assign null).

Therefore:

- Before `Game.init(...)` has been successfully called, `gameLoop` can still be its default value (null).
- After `Game.terminate()` has run, `gameLoop` is explicitly null.
- Between `init` and `terminate`, including while the game is running, `gameLoop` is non‑null.

The various call sites you provided (e.g. `GameWorld.loadEnvironment`, `Emitter.activate/deactivate`, `MovementController.handleMovement`, `GameTime.now/since`, GUI/event listeners, etc.) do not enforce that `Game.init()` has already been called and that `Game.terminate()` has not yet run. Many of these calls are on UI events or entity constructors that can, in principle, be invoked at times when:

- the game has not yet been initialized, or
- the game has been terminated (e.g., shutdown hook), or
- some external code is still calling into the API after termination.

In particular, the library itself is aware that `loop()` can be null, as evidenced by multiple defensive checks:

```java
final IGameLoop loop = Game.loop();
if (loop != null) {
  loop.attach(this);
}
```

and

```java
final IGameLoop loop = Game.loop();
if (loop != null) {
  this.lastScreenChange = loop.getTicks();
}
```

If the authors considered `Game.loop()` to be non‑null at all times, these null checks would be unnecessary.

Given that:

- `Game.loop()` literally returns `gameLoop`,
- `gameLoop` is explicitly set to `null` in `terminate`, and
- there’s no global guarantee in the code you supplied that prevents any of the shown call sites from executing after `terminate` or before `init`,

it is possible for `Game.loop()` to return null at this call site in some program execution states (notably, pre‑init and post‑terminate). Thus the method can, in general, return null at this specific invocation.
